# Field Fault Tracker

## 1. Project Overview

Field Fault Tracker is a fault reporting and management system designed for field operations in power plants.

The system allows field technicians to report equipment faults and enables authorized users at the central management side to monitor, update, and manage these fault records.

The project is designed with an offline-capable mobile application in mind and a centralized backend and database architecture.

---

## 2. Project Purpose

The main purpose of the project is to digitize the fault reporting process between field technicians and the central management team.

The system aims to provide:

- Faster fault reporting
- Centralized fault tracking
- Equipment and plant-based fault management
- Technician assignment
- Fault status tracking
- Fault priority management
- Structured fault categories
- Future offline operation for field technicians
- A centralized management interface

---

## 3. System Architecture

The planned system consists of three main components:

```text
Flutter Mobile Application
          |
          v
      FastAPI Backend
          |
          v
   PostgreSQL Database

   Mobile Application

The mobile application will be developed using Flutter.

Field technicians will use the mobile application to create and manage fault reports from the field.

The application is planned to support offline operation and synchronization with the central system.

Backend

The backend is developed using Python and FastAPI.

The backend provides REST API endpoints for:

Plants
Equipment
Technicians
Fault categories
Fault records
Database

PostgreSQL is used as the relational database system.

The development environment uses PostgreSQL through Docker, while the production database is hosted on Neon PostgreSQL.

4. Technology Stack
Component	Technology
Backend	Python
API Framework	FastAPI
Database Access	SQLAlchemy
Database	PostgreSQL
Production Database	Neon PostgreSQL
Local Database	PostgreSQL Docker Container
Database Migration	Alembic
Mobile Application	Flutter
API Testing	Postman
Version Control	Git / GitHub
Deployment	Render
5. Database Structure

The database currently contains the following main tables:

Plants

Stores information about power plants.

Main fields:

id
name
type
province
installed_power_mw
commissioning_year
plant_code
Equipment

Stores equipment information associated with plants.

Main fields:

id
equipment_code
plant_id
name
type
criticality
plant_code
Technicians

Stores field technician information.

Main fields:

id
full_name
specialization
technician_code
main_plant_code
Fault Categories

Stores the categories used to classify faults.

Main fields:

id
category_code
name
description
Faults

Stores fault reports created by technicians.

Main fields:

id
client_uuid
equipment_id
category_id
technician_id
title
description
status
priority
created_at
updated_at
6. Database Relationships

The main relationships are:

Plant
  |
  +---- Equipment
             |
             +---- Fault
                    |
                    +---- Fault Category
                    |
                    +---- Technician

An equipment item belongs to a plant.

A fault can be associated with an equipment item, a fault category, and a technician.

7. Development Process

The project was developed incrementally.

Phase 1 - Project Planning

The initial project requirements and architecture were defined.

The main requirements were identified as:

Field fault reporting
Central management
Equipment tracking
Technician assignment
Fault categorization
Offline capability
Centralized database
Phase 2 - Database Design

The required database entities were identified.

The initial database structure contained:

Plants
Equipment
Technicians
Fault Categories
Faults

Relationships between these entities were defined using foreign keys.

Phase 3 - Local PostgreSQL Environment

A PostgreSQL database was created locally using Docker.

The local database configuration was:

Database: saha_ariza
User: admin
Port: 5432

The Docker container was configured as:

saha_ariza_postgres

This environment was used for local development and testing.

Phase 4 - Backend Development

The FastAPI backend was created.

The backend was configured to communicate with PostgreSQL using SQLAlchemy.

The following API endpoints were implemented:

GET    /health
GET    /db-test

GET    /plants
GET    /equipment
GET    /technicians
GET    /fault-categories

POST   /faults
GET    /faults
GET    /faults/{fault_id}

PUT    /faults/{fault_id}
DELETE /faults/{fault_id}

These endpoints provide the basic CRUD functionality required for fault management.

Phase 5 - Database Migration

Alembic was introduced for database schema management.

The database tables were migrated from the initial Turkish naming structure to an English naming structure.

The final table names are:

plants
equipment
technicians
fault_categories
faults
Phase 6 - Production Database

Neon PostgreSQL was selected as the production database.

A separate Alembic migration was created for the Neon database.

The production schema was successfully created.

Local development data was then transferred to Neon.

The transferred data included:

16 plants
98 equipment records
8 technicians
6 fault categories
Fault records from the local database
Phase 7 - Backend Deployment

The FastAPI backend was deployed using Render.

The production architecture became:

Client
  |
  v
Render
  |
  v
FastAPI
  |
  v
Neon PostgreSQL

The backend was tested against the Neon database.

Database connectivity and plant data retrieval were successfully verified.

8. Current API Functionality

The backend currently supports fault creation, listing, viewing, updating, and deletion.

Create Fault
POST /faults

Creates a new fault record.

List Faults
GET /faults

Returns fault records together with related equipment, technician, and category information.

Get Fault
GET /faults/{fault_id}

Returns detailed information about a specific fault.
Update Fault
PUT /faults/{fault_id}

Updates:

Title
Description
Status
Priority
Equipment
Category
Technician
Delete Fault
DELETE /faults/{fault_id}

Deletes a fault record.

9. Data Migration

The initial database used Turkish table names.

The database structure was migrated to English table names to provide a consistent naming convention across the backend and database.

The migration process preserved the existing data.

The production Neon database was populated using the local PostgreSQL data.

The migration was verified by checking the record counts in the production database.

